1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,556
You built a jar. You gave it to the operations team. They said, we cannot deploy that.

2
00:00:06,106 --> 00:00:08,230
A web application is not a jar.

3
00:00:08,230 --> 00:00:11,588
A server like tomcat expects a different package,

4
00:00:11,588 --> 00:00:11,999
a war,

5
00:00:11,999 --> 00:00:13,850
with a fixed layout inside.

6
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:19,712
Today you package cloud cart as a war, open it up, and check exactly what is inside.

7
00:00:20,262 --> 00:00:23,441
Then you decide which libraries go in, and which stay out.

8
00:00:23,991 --> 00:00:25,011
One honest note.

9
00:00:25,011 --> 00:00:28,134
My recording computer cannot reach Maven Central,

10
00:00:28,134 --> 00:00:31,193
so the libraries come from a small local folder.

11
00:00:31,193 --> 00:00:34,508
The commands and the contents are what you will see.

12
00:00:36,058 --> 00:00:40,517
A war is a web archive. It is a zip file, with the extension dot war.

13
00:00:41,067 --> 00:00:43,167
You hand it to a servlet container,

14
00:00:43,167 --> 00:00:44,067
such as tomcat.

15
00:00:44,067 --> 00:00:46,827
The container unpacks it and serves your site.

16
00:00:47,377 --> 00:00:48,852
A jar holds classes.

17
00:00:48,852 --> 00:00:52,023
A war holds a whole web application: pages,

18
00:00:52,023 --> 00:00:52,687
settings,

19
00:00:52,687 --> 00:00:54,310
classes and libraries.

20
00:00:54,860 --> 00:00:59,831
In Maven you do not run a new tool. You change one line in the pom: the packaging.

21
00:01:00,381 --> 00:01:03,943
Packaging jar, makes a jar. Packaging war, makes a war.

22
00:01:05,993 --> 00:01:09,726
A war has a strict layout. The container expects it exactly.

23
00:01:10,276 --> 00:01:15,012
Files in the top folder, like index dot H T M L, are public pages.

24
00:01:15,562 --> 00:01:19,040
The folder web inf is private. Browsers can never reach it.

25
00:01:19,590 --> 00:01:21,894
Inside web inf: web dot X M L,

26
00:01:21,894 --> 00:01:22,892
the settings.

27
00:01:22,892 --> 00:01:23,506
Classes,

28
00:01:23,506 --> 00:01:24,966
your compiled code.

29
00:01:24,966 --> 00:01:25,580
And lib,

30
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:26,886
the library jars.

31
00:01:27,436 --> 00:01:31,560
Maven builds this layout for you from your source folders: source,

32
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:31,872
main,

33
00:01:31,872 --> 00:01:32,310
webapp,

34
00:01:32,310 --> 00:01:35,372
plus your compiled classes and your dependencies.

35
00:01:37,422 --> 00:01:40,473
Which libraries go in the war? That depends on the scope.

36
00:01:41,023 --> 00:01:46,996
Compile scope, the default. Needed to build and to run. Maven copies it into web inf lib.

37
00:01:47,546 --> 00:01:53,114
Provided scope. Needed to build, but the server already has it. Maven does not package it.

38
00:01:53,664 --> 00:01:56,032
A classic example is the servlet API.

39
00:01:56,032 --> 00:01:57,696
The container supplies it.

40
00:01:57,696 --> 00:01:59,360
If you pack your own copy,

41
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:00,960
you can break the server.

42
00:02:01,510 --> 00:02:04,859
So the rule: when the server supplies it, mark it provided.

43
00:02:06,909 --> 00:02:10,066
Go to the demo project. Type: CD, demo.

44
00:02:10,616 --> 00:02:17,187
First, build it as it is. Type: mvn -B -q clean package && ls target

45
00:02:17,317 --> 00:02:19,557
Quiet mode, so only problems print.

46
00:02:26,107 --> 00:02:31,952
A jar. Cart web, one point zero point zero, dot jar. Not deployable on a server.

47
00:02:32,502 --> 00:02:36,470
Change the packaging. Type: sed -i 's/<packaging>jar/<packaging>war/' pom.xml

48
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:43,794
Name the file. Type: sed -i '/<build>/a  <finalName>cloudcart</finalName>' pom.xml

49
00:02:43,924 --> 00:02:45,822
Build again. Type: mvn -B -q clean package && ls target

50
00:02:47,952 --> 00:02:49,531
Same command as before.

51
00:02:56,081 --> 00:03:00,412
Now there is a cloud cart dot war. And a folder with the unpacked version.

52
00:03:00,962 --> 00:03:05,890
Look inside. Type: unzip -Z1 target/cloudcart.war | grep -v '/$'

53
00:03:06,020 --> 00:03:06,662
The page,

54
00:03:06,662 --> 00:03:07,447
at the top.

55
00:03:07,447 --> 00:03:09,303
In web inf: web dot X M L,

56
00:03:09,303 --> 00:03:11,016
the compiled cart class,

57
00:03:11,016 --> 00:03:13,870
and two jars in lib: you till and lib A.

58
00:03:14,420 --> 00:03:15,988
But lib A is supplied by the server.

59
00:03:15,988 --> 00:03:16,729
Mark it provided.

60
00:03:16,729 --> 00:03:19,647
Type: sed -i '/<artifactId>liba/a  <scope>provided</scope>' pom.xml

61
00:03:20,777 --> 00:03:21,438
Rebuild and list again.

62
00:03:21,438 --> 00:03:23,678
Type: mvn -B -q clean package && unzip -Z1 target/cloudcart.war | grep -v '/$'

63
00:03:27,608 --> 00:03:28,568
Almost done.

64
00:03:36,118 --> 00:03:36,966
Lib A is gone.

65
00:03:36,966 --> 00:03:38,601
Only you till stays in lib.

66
00:03:38,601 --> 00:03:39,752
The war is smaller,

67
00:03:39,752 --> 00:03:41,750
and cannot clash with the server.

